{
  "_about": "Component spec for pos-toast (style/pos-toast.css + partials/toasts.liquid + assets/js/pos-toast.js). Built 2026-09-03 from the real, already-shipped CSS/JS — style/pos-toast.css already existed (136 lines) before this spec, just never had a Tokens-panel spec, docs page, or nav wiring beyond a coming-soon badge placeholder. The 'info' variant (.pos-toast-info) previously used --pos-color-warning/--pos-color-warning-muted by mistake (a real pre-existing bug in the legacy CSS, not a spec error) — fixed 2026-09-03 to use the same neutral/grey treatment as the Info alert card (--pos-color-highlight-background/--pos-color-frame/--pos-color-content-text-supplementary). Four types: success/error/info/warning (warning added 2026-09-03, see below). The close button (.pos-toast-close) has no :focus-visible styling anywhere in the CSS — a real, pre-existing accessibility gap, flagged as a known gap, not invented here. The toast message text has no color rule of its own in this component's CSS — it inherits --pos-color-content-text from the app-root .pos-app rule (pos-page.css). Listed under shared.text for completeness even though it is ambient, not component-local. The outer .pos-toasts container's own fixed positioning (width, inset, stacking gap) is deliberately excluded from this spec, same as the established instance-to-instance-gap exclusion (RULES.md §7.6, --pos-gap-card-card example) — it describes where the one shared container sits on the page, not the individual toast message being documented here. Regenerate spec-toast.liquid via the Node regen snippet (RULES.md §6.4) if this file changes. The close button (×) color was unified 2026-09-03 across all three intents — previously each intent tinted it with its own utility color at rest and all three faded to plain content-text on hover; now it always uses the shared subdued icon-interactive token at rest and the shared plain interactive token on hover, matching the standard icon-signals-interactivity pattern used elsewhere in the DS (RULES.md §4.10). 2026-09-03: added the real fourth Warning type (pos-toast.js accepts any string type; only the CSS/spec previously limited docs to three) — background --pos-color-warning-muted, same shared border/icon/text/layout/elevation as the other three intents. The toast border is no longer tinted per intent — it now uses the shared --pos-color-frame-transparent token (invisible in light/dark, visible in HC), same as Card/Alert card, and every toast now carries the standard medium elevation shadow (--pos-primitive-shadow-medium). 2026-09-03: the close button switched from a literal × character to the real x icon SVG, sized with --pos-primitive-size-125 (10px), matching Tag’s remove icon (also reduced to the same size) that the two be visually equal. 2026-09-03: the close button now composes the shared .pos-close component (pos-close.css) instead of duplicating its own color/hover CSS — fixes two issues found in review: the icon was misaligned (align-self: start left over from when it was a text glyph, not centered like a fixed-size SVG) and missing .pos-close's hover scale-up (1.2x), which Tag's own remove button already gets via the same shared class. Fixed to align-self: center and to compose .pos-close directly (.pos-toast-close.pos-close compound selector, so its own padding/margin/font-size overrides always win over .pos-close's regardless of stylesheet load order). 2026-09-03: the close animation now uses the shared .pos-anim-dismiss--out (pos-behaviors.css, fade + scale down to 0.85) instead of the old bespoke slide-left-and-collapse keyframes — pos-toast.js adds both pos-toast-unloading (legacy class name, kept for backward compatibility; style/pos-toast.css no longer defines it) and pos-anim-dismiss--out (the animation that actually plays). 2026-09-04: composedOf also lists control-icons — the close (×) button composes the shared .pos-close component, which has its own dedicated spec/Tokens panel (control-icons.json); the color tokens stay directly listed here too since they drive this component's own Normal/Hover toggle, but composedOf now correctly points to where the icon's full detail (including states not relevant here) actually lives. 2026-09-04: matched the real Figma toast component — added a bold message text weight (--pos-font-weight-bold, was unset/inherited-normal), and an optional severity badge icon (checkBadge/errorBadge/info/warning, new errorBadge icon derived from checkBadge's outer seal path + warning's exclamation glyph, both already-licensed assets) toggled via a new `icon` setting on window.pos.modules.toast() (default false, so existing callers render unchanged) and mirrored in the docs preview as an Options-panel switch-card. The close (×) button stays present even with the icon enabled — this diverges intentionally from the literal Figma mockup, which shows no close button at all. 2026-09-04: added requiresSelector to the severity badge icon color tokens so the Tokens panel correctly adds/removes them when the Options-panel Icon switch is toggled (previously showed regardless of toggle state).",
  "component": "toast",
  "name": "Toast",
  "description": "A floating, auto-dismissing notification confirming the result of an action (success, error, info, warning), stacked bottom-left on the page.",
  "whenToUse": [
    "Confirming the result of a user action (save, delete, submit) without interrupting their flow.",
    "Showing a brief success/error/info message that does not need to stay on screen or block interaction."
  ],
  "whenNotToUse": [
    "Showing a message that must stay visible in context next to specific content — use Alert card instead.",
    "Requiring the user to make a decision or acknowledge before continuing — use a Dialog instead."
  ],
  "do": [
    "Trigger via <code>new window.pos.modules.toast(type, message)</code> — never build the notification markup by hand.",
    "Use <code>success</code> for confirmations, <code>error</code> for failures, <code>info</code> for neutral updates.",
    "Keep the message short — one line, no more than a sentence."
  ],
  "dont": [
    "Stack more than a few toasts at once — they queue visually but too many becomes unreadable.",
    "Rely on a toast alone for critical/blocking errors the user must not miss — pair with inline validation or an Alert card too."
  ],
  "accessibility": [
    "Each toast has <code>role=\"alert\"</code> and <code>aria-live=\"assertive\"</code> so screen readers announce it immediately.",
    "The close button has a visually-hidden <code>.label</code> span with real text, not just the <code>×</code> glyph, for screen reader users."
  ],
